import json
S='styles'
def w(id,name,mo,fs,fe,regions,short,long,dc,status="historical",origin=None,decline=None):
    n={"id":id,"name":name,"rank":"family","member_of":mo,"status":status,
       "period":{"origin":origin,"floruit_start":fs,"floruit_end":fe,"decline_end":decline,"circa":True,"revival_periods":[]},
       "geography":{"regions":regions},"lineage":[],
       "description":{"short":short,"long":long},"defining_characteristics":dc,
       "confidence":"high","tags":["container"]}
    json.dump(n,open(f"{S}/{id}.json","w"),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)

F=[
("antique-classical","Antique Classical","classical-mediterranean",-600,400,["Greece","Italy","Roman Mediterranean"],
 "The Greek and Roman architecture of antiquity, in which the orders were first codified and the proportional method invented.",
 "Everything downstream in the classical stream is a reading of this material. Greek building gave the West a system of proportion derived from a module rather than from absolute measure, and a set of orders that graduate character from robust to delicate. Roman building gave it the arch, the vault, the dome, and a bureaucratic capacity to standardize and export the whole apparatus across three continents.\n\nWhat matters for a design system is that these are the only two bodies of ancient architecture the West has ever possessed complete enough to reverse-engineer. Every revival since the fifteenth century has worked from surviving Roman fabric and, after the 1750s, from measured Greek fabric — which is why the revivals differ so sharply in the accuracy of what they reproduce.",
 ["Proportional derivation from a module rather than absolute dimension","The orders as a graduated system of architectural character","Trabeated Greek construction and arcuated Roman construction as two distinct structural logics","Temple and domus as the two type-diagrams the West kept returning to"]),
("renaissance-classical","Renaissance Classical","classical-mediterranean",1420,1700,["Italy"],
 "The fifteenth- and sixteenth-century Italian recovery of antique proportion, and its conversion by Palladio into a transmissible design method.",
 "The Renaissance did not merely imitate Rome; it measured it, theorized it, and then printed it. That last step is the decisive one. Alberti, Serlio, Vignola, and above all Palladio converted a body of ruins into a set of rules a builder in another country and another century could execute from a plate.\n\nThe Quattro Libri of 1570 is the direct ancestor of the pattern book, the plan book, and the parametric catalog. Anyone building a rules-based design system today is working in a tradition that begins here, and would do well to notice both what it achieved and where it went thin — Palladio's rules govern proportion beautifully and say almost nothing about how a house grows.",
 ["Antique proportion recovered by measurement rather than inherited by craft","The treatise as the primary medium of transmission","Harmonic and geometric room ratios governing plan as well as elevation","The villa as a complete diagram of dwelling, agriculture, and status"]),
("continental-baroque-neoclassical","Continental Baroque & Neoclassical","classical-mediterranean",1600,1900,["France","Italy","Continental Europe"],
 "The French-led sequence from Baroque grandeur through Neoclassical restraint to the Beaux-Arts system that trained a generation of American architects.",
 "France took the Italian classical inheritance and made it a state apparatus: an academy, a curriculum, a hierarchy of building types, and a method of composition taught by competition. The Baroque phase pursued theatrical spatial effect; the Neoclassical reaction pursued archaeological correctness and structural honesty; the Beaux-Arts synthesis systematized both into a teachable design process built on parti, poché, and axial hierarchy.\n\nFor American residential architecture this family matters chiefly through the Ecole des Beaux-Arts, where Richardson, Hunt, and hundreds of others trained. The eclectic revivals of 1880–1940 are not naive antiquarianism; they are the product of a rigorous compositional education applied to borrowed vocabularies.",
 ["Composition taught as a formal discipline: parti, axis, hierarchy, poché","State and academy as patrons and standard-setters","Archaeological correctness as a competing value against expressive effect","Building types codified and ranked"]),
("mediterranean-vernacular","Mediterranean Vernacular","classical-mediterranean",1200,1900,["Tuscany","Italy","Mediterranean basin"],
 "The unwritten farmhouse and villa building of the Italian countryside, whose plain massing and tile roofs supplied the twentieth century with an entire romantic idiom.",
 "Alongside the treatise tradition ran a peasant one: stone or stuccoed masonry, low-pitched clay tile roofs, small deep-set openings, buildings that grew by accretion around a courtyard or along a slope. It has no theorists and no dates, and it produced the visual material that Italianate, Italian Renaissance Revival, and Mediterranean Revival all quarried.\n\nThe irony is instructive. The high tradition was designed to be copied and largely was not, outside of professional circles; the vernacular was never designed to be copied at all and became the most imitated Italian architecture in America.",
 ["Massive stuccoed or stone walls with deep reveals","Low-pitched clay tile roofs with modest overhang and exposed rafters","Accretive growth around courts and along contours","Ornament almost entirely absent, concentrated where it is unavoidable"]),

("medieval-british","Medieval British","british-isles",1066,1540,["England","Scotland","Wales"],
 "Norman masonry, English Gothic, and the timber-framed hall — the substrate every later British style either extended or rebelled against.",
 "The medieval inheritance in Britain is structural before it is stylistic: thick walls and round arches from Normandy, the pointed arch and its structural consequences from the twelfth century onward, and a parallel domestic tradition of oak framing that has almost nothing to do with either.\n\nThe domestic line matters most here. The open hall with its screens passage, the jettied upper floor, the cruck and box frame — these persist in ordinary building for centuries after the great churches stop being built, cross the Atlantic in the seventeenth century, and reappear as a fashion in the nineteenth.",
 ["Load-bearing masonry and oak box framing as two parallel construction cultures","The hall with screens passage as the governing domestic diagram","Steep roofs, small openings, and prominent chimney stacks","Craft transmission by guild and apprenticeship rather than by book"]),
("tudor-jacobean","Tudor & Jacobean","british-isles",1485,1625,["England"],
 "The transitional century in which English building acquired Renaissance ornament without surrendering its medieval plan and silhouette.",
 "Between the end of the Wars of the Roses and the arrival of Inigo Jones, English architecture took on classical motifs — pilasters, strapwork, shaped gables — while keeping medieval massing, medieval framing, and the medieval hall. The result is a hybrid that later purists disliked and that later revivalists loved precisely because of its irregularity.\n\nThe transmission channel is worth noting: the ornament arrived largely through Netherlandish engravings and immigrant craftsmen, not from Italy directly. It is a second-hand Renaissance, which explains its cheerful indifference to proportional correctness.",
 ["Renaissance ornament applied to medieval massing and plan","Tall multi-flued chimney stacks and large mullioned, transomed windows","Strapwork and shaped gables transmitted via the Low Countries","The H- and E-plan great house with hall at the centre"]),
("english-classical","English Classical","british-isles",1620,1840,["England","Scotland","Ireland"],
 "Two centuries of English classicism, from Jones and Wren through the Palladian, Georgian, Adam, and Regency phases, transmitted worldwide by pattern book.",
 "This family is the single most consequential body of work in the taxonomy for American residential architecture. It establishes the terms — symmetry, bay rhythm, the double-hung sash, storey graduation, the classical entry surround — that American building has been arguing with for three hundred years.\n\nIts historical significance is also methodological. The English carpenter's guide converted architectural design from a guild secret into a purchasable commodity. Gibbs, Langley, Swan, Salmon, and Halfpenny made it possible for a builder who had never seen a correct Ionic order to execute one. That achievement, and its characteristic failure mode — correct details at wrong scales — is the closest historical analogue to what a design platform now attempts.",
 ["Strict bilateral symmetry and vertically aligned bays","Storey heights graduated rather than equal","The double-hung sash as the governing opening type","Classical apparatus concentrated at the entry and cornice","Pattern-book transmission of correct detail to non-architect builders"]),
("british-picturesque","British Picturesque & Revival","british-isles",1740,1910,["England","Scotland"],
 "The deliberate composition of irregular, historically allusive buildings for visual and emotional effect — the intellectual origin of nearly all Anglo-American suburban architecture.",
 "The picturesque begins as a theory of landscape and becomes a theory of building: that irregularity, variety, and historical association are legitimate architectural aims, and that a house may be composed like a painting. Once accepted, it licenses Gothic Revival, Scottish Baronial, Old English, Queen Anne, and eventually the entire vocabulary of the freestanding suburban house.\n\nThis family is where the classical assumption that a building should be symmetrical is abandoned as a general rule. Everything that follows in America — the Victorian villa, the Shingle Style, the ranch — depends on that permission having been granted.",
 ["Asymmetry and irregular massing as deliberate compositional choices","Historical association valued as a source of architectural meaning","Silhouette and skyline treated as primary design material","Texture and material variety used to animate surface"]),
("british-arts-and-crafts","British Arts and Crafts","british-isles",1860,1915,["England"],
 "The reform movement that rejected industrial ornament in favour of honest material, visible craft, and the dignity of the vernacular cottage.",
 "Morris, Webb, Voysey, Lethaby, and Lutyens argued that the machine had corrupted both the object and the person who made it, and that architecture should return to local material, direct construction, and the modest forms of the rural cottage. The argument was moral before it was aesthetic.\n\nIt is the most philosophically explicit family in the taxonomy, and the one whose ideas travelled furthest — to Stickley's Craftsman in America, to the Deutscher Werkbund, and by an unexpected route into modernism, which kept the honesty-of-material argument while discarding the craft.",
 ["Material honesty: construction expressed rather than concealed","Local and traditional materials preferred to industrial substitutes","The vernacular cottage taken seriously as a model for serious architecture","Ornament arising from craft process rather than applied from a catalog","An explicit moral argument connecting how things are made to how people live"]),
("british-vernacular","British Vernacular","british-isles",1400,1900,["England","Wales"],
 "The unselfconscious regional building of the English countryside — Cotswold limestone, thatch, cob, and brick — later mined by revivalists for a whole century of cottage imagery.",
 "Regional vernacular in Britain is defined by geology. The Cotswolds build in oolitic limestone with stone-slate roofs because that is what the ground yields; East Anglia builds in flint and timber; the West Country in cob. Each produces a distinct language of wall thickness, roof pitch, opening size, and colour without any of it being designed.\n\nThe revival of this material from the 1890s onward — and its export to America as the Cotswold cottage and the Storybook style — is a case worth studying closely: a building culture generated entirely by local constraint, reproduced in places where none of those constraints apply.",
 ["Material determined by local geology, and everything else following from it","Steep roofs sized to the covering material — thatch, stone slate, or clay tile","Small irregular openings and very thick walls","Growth by accretion, producing irregular plans and rooflines","No designer, no date, no name"]),

("low-countries-vernacular","Low Countries Vernacular","northern-european-vernacular",1400,1900,["Netherlands","Belgium","South Africa"],
 "Dutch and Flemish brick building — the gabled canal house, the farmhouse, and their remarkable export to the Hudson Valley and the Cape of Good Hope.",
 "The Low Countries developed a brick urbanism of extraordinary discipline: narrow deep lots, party walls, and an elaborated street gable carrying all the architectural expression. On the land, a farmhouse tradition of large roof volumes over combined house and byre.\n\nThe export history is the interesting part. Dutch settlement carried this material to two places with almost nothing else in common — the Hudson and Delaware valleys, where it produced the American Dutch Colonial and its gambrel, and the Cape, where it met French Huguenot craft and Southeast Asian labour to produce Cape Dutch, one of the most distinctive hybrid architectures in the world.",
 ["Brick as the default material, with the gable as the site of expression","Narrow deep urban lots with party-wall construction","Large roof volumes doing significant work in the section","Whitewashed lime render in the colonial branches","Export by settlement rather than by treatise"]),
("germanic-vernacular","Germanic Vernacular","northern-european-vernacular",1200,1900,["Germany","Austria","Pennsylvania"],
 "The half-timbered Fachwerk town house and the great hall-house of the north German plain, and their descendants in Pennsylvania stone.",
 "German building culture is organized around the visible timber frame — an infilled skeleton whose geometry is the architecture — and around the integration of dwelling, storage, and livestock under a single very large roof. Both traits reflect a scarcity of good timber and an abundance of long winters.\n\nIn Pennsylvania this tradition met abundant limestone and reorganized itself: the frame went away, the massive wall arrived, and the bank house with its forebay carried the section logic of the German farm into an American landscape. The style disappeared within two generations; the section survived.",
 ["Expressed timber frame with infill panels as the primary wall system","House, byre, and storage integrated under one large roof","Very steep pitches sized to snow and to the roof's storage volume","Banked construction using slope for a sheltered lower level","Durable section logic surviving the loss of surface identity"]),
("french-vernacular","French Vernacular","northern-european-vernacular",1300,1900,["Normandy","Brittany","France"],
 "Norman half-timber, provincial stone farmhouse, and the small manoir — the source material for French Eclectic and Norman Revival in America.",
 "French regional building spans two very different worlds: the timber-framed, steeply-roofed farmhouses of Normandy, close cousins to English and German framing, and the stone farmhouse and manor of the interior with its hipped roofs, segmental arches, and characteristic round tower housing a stair.\n\nAmerican architects encountered this material directly — many of them served in France in the First World War and came home with sketchbooks. French Eclectic and the Norman cottage of the 1920s are among the very few American revival styles with a first-hand observational basis rather than a photographic one.",
 ["Steeply pitched hipped or gambrel roofs, often flared at the eave","Stucco, stone, or half-timber walls with segmental-arched openings","The round stair tower as a compositional and functional device","Farm buildings arranged around a court","Direct observational transmission to America after 1918"]),
("nordic-alpine-vernacular","Nordic & Alpine Vernacular","northern-european-vernacular",1100,1900,["Scandinavia","Switzerland","Alps"],
 "Log building and the deep-eaved chalet — timber traditions from regions with long winters, heavy snow, and abundant softwood.",
 "Horizontal log construction and the Alpine chalet share a logic: solid timber walls, very large roof overhangs, and elaborate carved detail concentrated on the gable and balcony where it can be seen and where the roof protects it.\n\nBoth reach America. Log building arrives with Finnish and Swedish settlers on the Delaware in 1638 and becomes the substrate of Appalachian building. The chalet arrives as a fashion in the 1850s through Downing and the pattern books, and its exposed rafter tails, brackets, and sawn balusters pass directly into the Craftsman bungalow half a century later.",
 ["Solid horizontal log or heavy timber walls rather than framed cavities","Very deep roof overhangs sheltering the wall and the balcony","Carved and sawn ornament concentrated at gable, bargeboard, and rail","Corner notching and joinery expressed as the primary detail","Span limited by available log length, setting room size directly"]),

("iberian-islamic","Iberian Islamic","iberian-mediterranean",750,1614,["Al-Andalus","Spain"],
 "Eight centuries of Islamic building in Iberia and its continuation as Mudejar craft under Christian rule.",
 "Al-Andalus produced an architecture organized around the courtyard, water, shade, and geometric surface — a set of moves for hot-dry climates that has never been improved on. Its influence on Spanish building did not end with the Reconquest; Muslim craftsmen continued working for Christian patrons for centuries, a tradition called Mudejar.\n\nThat continuity is a transmission edge, not an imitation. The same guilds, the same masons, the same techniques — only the patron changed. It is the clearest case in the taxonomy of a craft tradition outliving the culture that produced it, and a caution against reading architectural lineage off political history.",
 ["Courtyard and water as the organizing elements of the plan","Blind or minimally-opened street elevation","Geometric surface ornament in tile, brick, and carved plaster","Horseshoe and multifoil arches, intricate timber ceilings","Craft continuity across a political and religious rupture"]),
("spanish-classical","Spanish Classical","iberian-mediterranean",1490,1800,["Spain","Mexico","Spanish Americas"],
 "Plateresque and Churrigueresque — Spanish Renaissance and Baroque, in which classical structure carries an ornament of unmatched density.",
 "Spanish classicism concentrates rather than distributes. A plain wall of great expanse is punctuated by a single entry or retable of overwhelming elaboration — the strategy is one of contrast, not of even articulation, and it is the opposite of the French or English classical habit.\n\nCarried to the Americas, this strategy suited a labour economy in which skilled carving was available for a single focal point and nothing else. The result — vast plain adobe or stone walls with one intensely worked doorway — became the defining move of Spanish Colonial Revival four centuries later.",
 ["Ornament concentrated at a single focal point against large plain surfaces","Classical structure carrying non-classical densities of decoration","The retable façade as the organizing device","Adaptation to labour economies where skilled carving is scarce"]),
("iberian-vernacular","Iberian Vernacular","iberian-mediterranean",1200,1900,["Andalusia","Spain","Portugal"],
 "The whitewashed courtyard house of southern Spain — thick walls, small openings, deep shade, and a patio that does the work of a room.",
 "The Andalusian house is the Islamic courtyard diagram sustained in ordinary building for a thousand years: a blank whitewashed street wall, a controlled entry, and an interior court planted and often fitted with water. The climate logic is exact — thermal mass, night flushing, shaded transitional space, minimized solar gain on the envelope.\n\nThis is the most technically relevant traditional inheritance available for the American Sun Belt, and it is almost entirely unexploited by production builders there, who instead build Midwestern houses with more insulation.",
 ["Blank whitewashed street elevation and one controlled entry","Interior patio as the principal living space for much of the year","Very thick lime-rendered masonry with deep reveals","Small high openings and generous shaded galleries","Water and planting as architectural rather than decorative elements"]),
("colonial-iberian-americas","Colonial Iberian Americas","iberian-mediterranean",1520,1910,["Mexico","Central America","Southwest United States"],
 "Three centuries of Spanish building in the Americas, where Iberian plans met indigenous labour, adobe, and a continent of new conditions.",
 "Spanish colonial building in the Americas is a translation problem worked out at scale over three hundred years: European plan diagrams executed by indigenous craftsmen in local materials, under a Church that wanted magnificence and a Crown that would not pay for it.\n\nWhat emerged — massive plain walls, courtyards, arcaded corridors, tile roofs, and ornament concentrated at the portal — is the direct ancestor of the American Southwest's regional architecture, and by way of Mission Revival and Spanish Colonial Revival, of a substantial fraction of everything built in California, Arizona, Texas, and Florida since 1915.",
 ["European plan diagrams executed in local material by local labour","Courtyard and arcaded corredor as the organizing spatial devices","Massive adobe or rubble walls, plain except at the portal","Low-pitched clay tile roofs and long horizontal parapets","A genuinely new regional language rather than a diluted European one"]),

("american-colonial","American Colonial","north-american",1607,1780,["United States","Canada"],
 "The first American architecture: four European building cultures transplanted to four different climates, each mutating within two generations.",
 "The colonial period is best read as a controlled experiment. English, Dutch, German, French, and Spanish settlers each brought a coherent building tradition and each met conditions their tradition had not anticipated — colder winters, hotter summers, different timber, no guild structure, and a labour economy in which skilled craft was scarce and land was not.\n\nWhat survived the transplant is instructive and consistent: the section survived, the plan survived, the surface did not. Roof pitch, chimney position, ceiling height, and the relationship to grade persisted for centuries. Ornament vanished almost immediately and returned only when pattern books arrived in the 1730s.\n\nThe variant level is where this family becomes operationally useful. New England Georgian and Tidewater Georgian are the same style and different buildings, and the differences — cladding, chimney position, roof pitch, plan depth — are climatic and quantifiable.",
 ["Transplanted European traditions adapting to unanticipated climates","Structural and planning logic surviving where surface ornament did not","Scarcity of skilled craft producing simplification within a generation","Strong regional divergence within each national tradition","Pattern-book classicism arriving late and unevenly after about 1730"]),
("early-republic","Early Republic","north-american",1780,1860,["United States"],
 "Federal, Jeffersonian, and Greek Revival — the one period when American architecture was explicitly and self-consciously ideological.",
 "For roughly seventy years after independence, Americans chose architectural styles as political arguments. Federal continued English Adamesque practice while the country was still culturally British. Jefferson deliberately bypassed England to quote Rome and Palladio directly, because a republic should look to a republic. Greek Revival, arriving with Greek independence and Jacksonian democracy, made the temple front a national form and put it on farmhouses, banks, and courthouses alike.\n\nThe mechanism was the American pattern book, and the mechanism has a name: Asher Benjamin published *The American Builder's Companion* in 1806 as a Federal manual and *The Architect, or Practical House Carpenter* in 1830 as a Grecian one. Same author, same audience, same carpenters — new plates. Style change here is a change in the reference material distributed to the trade, which is worth sitting with if you are building the next reference material.",
 ["Style chosen as explicit political and civic argument","Pattern books as the operative mechanism of stylistic change","A decisive shift from Federal attenuation to Greek Revival mass and weight","The temple front applied across every building type without embarrassment","Classical correctness pursued from published plates rather than from travel"]),
("romantic-revivals","Romantic Revivals","north-american",1830,1890,["United States"],
 "Gothic, Italianate, and their companions — the moment when American architecture abandoned classical symmetry for association, irregularity, and moral argument.",
 "Andrew Jackson Downing's argument, made in books that sold enormously between 1842 and 1852, was that a house shapes the character of the family inside it, that the classical box was inappropriate to domestic life, and that irregular, picturesque, vine-covered houses in Gothic or Italian dress would produce better people. It is the first fully articulated American theory of domestic architecture, and it is a moral theory.\n\nThe enabling condition was technological. Balloon framing, machine-cut nails, and the steam scroll saw arrived in the 1830s and 1840s and made complex irregular massing and elaborate sawn ornament affordable for the first time in history. Downing supplied the argument; the sawmill supplied the means.",
 ["Irregular, asymmetrical massing composed for picturesque effect","Historical association as the source of architectural meaning","Balloon framing and machine-cut ornament as the enabling technology","An explicit moral claim that domestic architecture forms character","Vertical emphasis and steep rooflines replacing the classical horizontal"]),
("victorian","Victorian","north-american",1855,1910,["United States","Canada"],
 "The high era of American picturesque: Second Empire, Stick, Queen Anne, Richardsonian, Shingle, and the Folk Victorian that carried it to every small town.",
 "The Victorian styles share a method rather than a vocabulary: a dominant mass, a complex silhouette, multiple wall textures, and ornament used to animate surface rather than to articulate structure. What changes between them is which historical wardrobe is drawn on and how much of it.\n\nTwo nodes in this family carry outsized importance. Shingle Style is the taxonomy's best reticulation case — at least three genuine parents, none dominant. Folk Victorian is the mass-market case: vernacular massing plus catalog-ordered spindlework shipped by rail, ornament fully decoupled from structure and geography. Folk Victorian is the nineteenth century rehearsing exactly the problem a kit-of-parts platform now faces.",
 ["A dominant mass with a deliberately complex silhouette","Multiple wall textures within a single elevation","Ornament as surface animation rather than structural articulation","Catalog and railroad distribution decoupling ornament from place","Rapid stylistic turnover driven by fashion and by publication"]),
("eclectic-revivals","Eclectic Revivals","north-american",1880,1945,["United States"],
 "The period-revival era, in which professionally trained architects reproduced historical styles with increasing archaeological accuracy — and gave American suburbia its permanent vocabulary.",
 "Every style in this family claims a historical ancestor while descending from something else entirely: Beaux-Arts professional training, the measured-drawing and architectural-photography culture of the 1890s onward, and the millwork catalog. The gap between what these styles claim and where they actually come from is the most important fact about them, and the reason the taxonomy needs separate `references` and `descends_from` edges.\n\nAccuracy increases sharply after about 1915, as measured drawings of colonial buildings circulate in portfolios like the White Pine Series. Early Colonial Revival is a free interpretation at exaggerated scale; late Colonial Revival is nearly a reproduction. A builder needs to know which one is being asked for, and the distinction is invisible if the taxonomy stops at the style name.\n\nThis is also where the contemporary American production-home vocabulary actually comes from. Almost every traditional plan sold today is a descendant of something in this family rather than of anything genuinely colonial.",
 ["Historical accuracy as an explicit and increasing professional value","Beaux-Arts compositional training applied to borrowed vocabularies","Photography, measured drawings, and portfolios as the transmission medium","Systematic departure from the model in scale, storey height, and bay width","The origin of nearly all current American traditional production housing"]),
("american-arts-and-crafts","American Arts and Crafts","north-american",1900,1935,["United States"],
 "Craftsman, bungalow, and Prairie — the American translation of the British reform argument into a mass-market house that millions actually bought.",
 "Britain produced the philosophy and a few hundred houses for wealthy clients. America produced hundreds of thousands of houses and shipped them by catalog. Gustav Stickley's *The Craftsman* magazine ran from 1901 to 1916 and distributed plans directly to readers; Sears, Aladdin, and dozens of plan services did the rest.\n\nThe result is the only moment in American history when a reform movement grounded in material honesty and craft became the default choice of ordinary buyers. It ended for economic reasons — the bungalow's exposed structure and heavy timber were expensive, and the Depression killed it — not because anyone argued it down.",
 ["Structure expressed rather than concealed: rafter tails, brackets, beams, joinery","Low horizontal massing with very deep eave overhangs","The porch under the primary roof as an outdoor room","Open planning around a hearth, replacing the formal parlor","Mass distribution by magazine and mail-order catalog"]),
("mid-century-traditional","Mid-Century Traditional","north-american",1930,1975,["United States"],
 "Minimal Traditional, Ranch, and their relatives — the era when the traditional kit of parts was systematically cost-engineered out of the American house.",
 "This family is the most important and least examined in the whole taxonomy for anyone building production housing. Minimal Traditional keeps the traditional silhouette and deletes everything else: no overhang, no cornice return, no depth of profile, sash reduced to the cheapest available. It was underwritten by FHA lending standards that rewarded minimum cost per square foot and had no way to value proportion.\n\nThe Ranch that follows is the first American house organized around the automobile, and it inherits an unsolved problem that every traditional style built today still carries: how to put two or three garage doors on a street elevation without destroying it. No historical style answers this, because none had to. It is the single largest gap in the traditional kit of parts and the place where a modern design system has to author something genuinely new rather than retrieve it.",
 ["Traditional silhouette retained while trim, overhang, and profile depth are deleted","Financing standards and cost per square foot as the governing design constraint","The automobile reorganizing plan, lot, and street elevation","Horizontal single-storey massing on wide suburban lots","An unresolved garage problem inherited by everything built since"]),
("contemporary-traditional","Contemporary Traditional","north-american",1980,2026,["United States"],
 "New Classical, New Urbanist, and the current market idioms — the living attempt to recover traditional design logic under contemporary economics.",
 "Since about 1980 a serious body of practice has worked to recover traditional architecture as a living discipline rather than a costume: Krier's urban theory, Duany and Plater-Zyberk's codes at Seaside and Kentlands, the Institute of Classical Architecture and Art, and the Notre Dame school.\n\nThe striking thing is the mechanism. These practitioners returned to pattern books and form-based codes — the eighteenth-century solution — because they faced the eighteenth-century problem: how to get correct traditional building out of a trade that no longer carries the knowledge. That is the immediate precedent for encoding design logic in software, and the reason this family sits at the end of the taxonomy rather than as an appendix to it.",
 ["Deliberate recovery of traditional design logic rather than of style alone","Pattern books and form-based codes revived as the transmission mechanism","Urbanism treated as inseparable from architecture","Contemporary programme, construction, and code accommodated openly","An explicit argument with the market idioms it competes against"],"living"),
("american-folk-vernacular","American Folk Vernacular","north-american",1700,1930,["United States"],
 "Log, shotgun, farmhouse, and the other unstyled house types that most Americans actually lived in.",
 "Most American houses were never designed and never carried a style name. They were built from types — a log pen, a double pen, a shotgun, a gable-front-and-wing farmhouse — that encode generations of accumulated judgment about span, heating, ventilation, and how a family grows.\n\nTreating this family as residual would be a mistake. These types are the substrate the styled architecture sits on, they are the direct ancestors of the current market's most popular idiom, and their expansion logic — how a house grows without breaking — is the property that formal styles most conspicuously lack and that a generative design system most needs.",
 ["Type rather than style as the unit of design","Structural and climatic logic governing form directly","Ornament minimal, applied late, and bought rather than made","Expansion and accretion as designed-in properties","Transmission entirely by craft memory and imitation"]),
]
for f in F: w(*f)
print("families:", len(F))
